import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';

// Unit economics — REAL, measured numbers only (the site's whole thesis is evidence
// over claims). Empty array renders nothing. A placeholder-shaped value fails the
// build, so a stub can never ship by accident.
interface EconRow { metric: string; value: string; basis: string; }
const econ: EconRow[] = [];
if (econ.some((r) => /TBD|XX+|placeholder|\?\?\?|\bN\/A\b|\bX (ms|tokens|per|in)\b/i.test(`${r.metric} ${r.value} ${r.basis}`))) {
  throw new Error('operation-hired econ: placeholder value present. Fill measured numbers or leave the array empty.');
}
---
<CaseStudy slug="operation-hired">
  <p class="lead reveal">I needed to run a serious job search, so I built the system I'd roll out to a team. Not a spreadsheet and not a folder of bookmarks: a pipeline that pulls postings in, scores them, drafts the application, and keeps a record of every send. The point was never the software. The point was to make one person move like a staffed function, without losing the judgment that makes the work any good.</p>

  <h2 class="reveal">A discovery board, not a job board</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Postings land in a local <code>SQLite</code> database and every one runs through a scoring model before I ever see it. So far it has scored <strong>14,800+</strong> of them, and each score can be reproduced from the source data. What surfaces is a ranked board carrying the signals I actually decide on:</p>
  <ul class="reveal">
    <li><strong>Tier.</strong> Postings get bucketed by fit: role, seniority, pay signal. An early version was too generous with the top bucket, so I tightened it. Tier 1 dropped from a few thousand to about a hundred, which is what real fit should look like.</li>
    <li><strong>Fraud.</strong> A set of heuristics flags the patterns ghost and scam listings share. That cut the flagged-fraud noise by about 80%, so the board stays honest.</li>
    <li><strong>Freshness.</strong> A sweep re-checks live postings against the source job board and is deliberately conservative: only a hard <code>404</code> or <code>410</code> retires a listing, while a timeout or a redirect is left alone so a real job is never dropped by mistake. The first sweep pulled 12 dead postings out of the pool, so I never spend a morning chasing a role that closed last week.</li>
  </ul>

  {econ.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h2 class="reveal">What a scored posting costs</h2>
      <div class="econ reveal">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Metric</th><th scope="col">Value</th><th scope="col">Basis</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {econ.map((r) => (
              <tr><td>{r.metric}</td><td class="econ__v">{r.value}</td><td>{r.basis}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 class="reveal">A fit report that surfaces gaps instead of faking them</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Pick a job and the system writes a fit report, then drafts a tailored package off it. The report grades every requirement into three honest tiers, and the third one is the whole point:</p>
  <ul class="reveal">
    <li><strong>Matched.</strong> Direct, provable overlap between the requirement and my record.</li>
    <li><strong>Equivalent.</strong> Different words, same capability, named explicitly so the mapping is a claim I can stand behind, not a stretch.</li>
    <li><strong>Gap.</strong> Where I don't line up, it says so. A tool that scored everyone a perfect fit would be worthless. This one surfaces gaps instead of faking them, which is the only version I'd let near a real decision.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="reveal">A human approves every send</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Nothing goes out on its own. Drafted packages sit in a submission ledger behind a funnel view, and every send is a deliberate human action: I read it, I approve it, it logs. The automation makes the human faster; it never replaces the human. That line, drawn on purpose, is what keeps the whole thing trustworthy:</p>
  <ul class="reveal">
    <li>A <strong>re-score pipeline</strong> re-ran every row (5,905 at the time) against the corrected model in one pass with <strong>zero errors</strong>, after backing up first.</li>
    <li>A <strong>test suite</strong> covers the scoring edges, the geo logic, and the fraud heuristics, and it's green before anything ships.</li>
    <li><strong>Data-hygiene guards</strong> clear out orphaned rows and stop half-formed records from moving down the pipeline.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="reveal">A follow-up ladder that never nags</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Applying is the first move; the follow-through is where most searches go quiet. Once a package is sent, the system opens a follow-up ladder anchored to that submission: a hiring-manager note on day zero, a check-in around day five, another near day fourteen. It logs only the touches I actually make, and when a window passes it marks it closed instead of firing a reminder on its own. The tool keeps the schedule; I make the contact.</p>

  <h2 id="guardrails" class="reveal">Guardrails I built in on purpose</h2>
  <p class="reveal">A system that reaches out to the internet and drafts on my behalf needs limits it cannot talk itself out of. Two of them do the heavy lifting:</p>
  <ul class="reveal">
    <li><strong>An SSRF-safe fetch.</strong> The freshness sweep pulls live URLs, so every request runs through a guarded fetch that only speaks <code>http</code> and <code>https</code>, refuses private, loopback, link-local, and cloud-metadata addresses, re-checks every redirect hop against the same rules, and rejects the IPv4-mapped-IPv6 tricks that slip past a naive block. A job board should never be able to point my own tool at an internal address.</li>
    <li><strong>An anti-fabrication grounding check.</strong> Before any generated answer goes near an application, it passes a grounding check that rejects any number, dollar figure, or credential that is not already in my own record, and falls back to a plain, grounded template instead. The tool can help me say what is true faster; it is structurally kept from inventing what is not.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="reveal">Why I point to this one</h2>
  <p class="reveal">That's what AI enablement means to me: real workflows, honest guardrails, and adoption you can trust. It's a system that makes a lot of decisions, knows where it might be wrong, and keeps a person in the loop on the ones that matter. I'd rather show evidence than make claims, and that holds for everything I build.</p>
</CaseStudy>
